---
import globalStyles from "../styles/global.css?raw";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Research clusters by Fernando Miramontes Forattini across governance, classification, inequality, technology, AI, and sociotechnical systems."
    />
    <title>Research | Fernando Miramontes Forattini</title>
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" sizes="any" href="/research-icon.svg?v=2" />
    <link rel="shortcut icon" href="/research-icon.svg?v=2" />
    <link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://fernandomforattini.com/research/" />
    <link rel="alternate" hreflang="pt-BR" href="https://fernandomforattini.com/pt/research/" />
    <link rel="alternate" hreflang="es-419" href="https://fernandomforattini.com/es/research/" />
    <style is:global set:html={globalStyles}></style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-header" aria-label="Site header">
      <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Fernando Miramontes Forattini homepage">
        <span class="brand-mark">Fernando Miramontes Forattini</span>
        <span class="brand-sub">Historical Sociology · Governance · Technology</span>
      </a>

      <nav class="site-nav" id="site-nav" data-site-nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="/research/">Research</a>
        <a href="/publications/">Publications</a>
        <a href="/#teaching">Teaching</a>
        <a href="/#projects">Applied Work</a>
        <a href="/cv/fernando-miramontes-forattini-cv-english.pdf" download>CV</a>
        <a href="/#contact">Contact</a>
      </nav>

      <div class="language-switcher" aria-label="Language selector">
        <a class="is-active" href="/research/" aria-current="page">EN</a>
        <a href="/pt/research/">PT</a>
        <a href="/es/research/">ES</a>
      </div>

      <button class="menu-button" type="button" data-menu-button aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="site-nav">
        <span class="menu-lines" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="sr-only">Open menu</span>
      </button>
    </header>

    <main class="subpage-main research-main">
      <section class="subpage-hero">
        <p class="section-kicker">Research</p>
        <h1 class="section-title">Three clusters, one question.</h1>
        <p class="section-copy">
          How do institutions, classifications, and technologies organize power, produce
          vulnerability, and reshape governance?
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="research-cluster-section" id="governance" aria-labelledby="governance-title">
        <div class="research-cluster-intro">
          <p class="section-kicker">Cluster 01</p>
          <h2 id="governance-title">Governance, corruption, and institutional power</h2>
          <p>
            This cluster examines how reforms, public services, state capacity, corruption politics,
            opacity, and institutional histories shape contemporary forms of governance.
          </p>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">Governance</span>
            <span class="tag">Corruption</span>
            <span class="tag">Historical Sociology</span>
            <span class="tag">Institutions</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <article class="research-featured-publication">
          <p class="meta">Featured publication · Cambridge University Press · 2026</p>
          <h3>
            Forattini, F. M. (2026). The contingency of mobilization: The social contexts of
            Operation Lava Jato in Brazil. In M. Garrido, M. Zaloznaya, &amp; N. H. Wilson
            (Eds.), <em>A comparative historical sociology of corruption</em>. Cambridge
            University Press.
          </h3>
          <p>
            A central statement of the governance/corruption/historical sociology axis.
          </p>
        </article>

        <div class="publication-list">
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">American Behavioral Scientist · 2026</p>
            <h3>Beyond “best practices”: A historical-sociological critique of privatization, decentralization, and anti-corruption reforms in 1990s Brazil.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">Governance</span>
              <span class="tag">Reform</span>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">Journal of Interdisciplinary History / MIT Press · 2026</p>
            <h3>The rise and evolution of anti-corruption impulses in the United States and Brazil.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">Corruption</span>
              <span class="tag">Comparative History</span>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">Palgrave Macmillan · 2026</p>
            <h3>Fighting sexual corruption in public services: Lessons from women’s experiences in Brazil.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">Public Services</span>
              <span class="tag">Gender</span>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>

        <a class="section-link" href="/publications/#governance">View all publications →</a>
      </section>

      <section class="research-cluster-section" id="classification" aria-labelledby="classification-title">
        <div class="research-cluster-intro">
          <p class="section-kicker">Cluster 02</p>
          <h2 id="classification-title">Inequality, classification, and recognition</h2>
          <p>
            This cluster studies how race, Indigeneity, identity categories, vulnerability, and
            official classifications mediate recognition, inequality, and political life.
          </p>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">Classification</span>
            <span class="tag">Inequality</span>
            <span class="tag">Indigeneity</span>
            <span class="tag">Recognition</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <article class="research-featured-publication">
          <p class="meta">Featured publication · Oxford University Press · 2026</p>
          <h3>
            Forattini, F., &amp; Miranda Braga, B. (2026). The weight of the “pardo”
            category: Official statistics and the deracialization of Indigenous peoples.
            In <em>Oxford Intersections: Racism by Context</em>. Oxford University Press.
          </h3>
          <p>
            A core piece on race, classification, Indigenous recognition, and data inequality.
          </p>
        </article>

        <div class="publication-list">
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">Cronos · 2023</p>
            <h3>Produção e reprodução das desigualdades sociais relacionadas à COVID-19 expressas nas redes sociais.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">Inequality</span>
              <span class="tag">Social Data</span>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">European Review of Latin American and Caribbean Studies · 2020</p>
            <h3>Piripkura: The plight of Indigenous people, environment, and Brazilian identity.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">Indigeneity</span>
              <span class="tag">Environment</span>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">Culture, identity, and political imagination</p>
            <h3>Superman e a virada conservadora de Reagan.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">Culture</span>
              <span class="tag">Politics</span>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>

        <a class="section-link" href="/publications/#classification">View all publications →</a>
      </section>

      <section class="research-cluster-section" id="technology" aria-labelledby="technology-title">
        <div class="research-cluster-intro">
          <p class="section-kicker">Cluster 03</p>
          <h2 id="technology-title">Technology, AI, and sociotechnical governance</h2>
          <p>
            This cluster connects digital public services, responsible AI, care ethics, gendered
            harm, information systems, and institutional accountability.
          </p>
          <div class="tag-row">
            <span class="tag">Technology</span>
            <span class="tag">AI</span>
            <span class="tag">Gender</span>
            <span class="tag">Institutions</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <article class="research-featured-publication">
          <p class="meta">Featured publication · European Journal of Information Systems · under review</p>
          <h3>
            Forattini, F., &amp; Connolly, R. (under review). When Promethean leaps meet
            institutional power: Designing digital public services through a care-ethical lens.
            <em>European Journal of Information Systems</em>.
          </h3>
          <p>
            A bridge into information systems, digital public services, care ethics, and
            institutional power.
          </p>
        </article>

        <div class="publication-list">
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">Information Systems Management · 2026</p>
            <h3>Prometheus ambition, Zeusian order: Why leaps in information systems require structurally aware ethical frameworks.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">AI Ethics</span>
              <span class="tag">Institutions</span>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">ACM SIGMIS Database · 2026</p>
            <h3>From design to policy: Embedding care ethics and STEM institutional insights in gender-focused technology.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">Care Ethics</span>
              <span class="tag">Gender</span>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">ACM SIGMIS Database · 2025</p>
            <h3>Breaking boundaries: Advancing gender and technology research to combat sextortion.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">Technology</span>
              <span class="tag">Gendered Harm</span>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article class="publication-row">
            <p class="meta">HICSS · 2026</p>
            <h3>AI agents in gender-sensitive public service encounters.</h3>
            <div class="tag-row">
              <span class="tag">AI</span>
              <span class="tag">Public Services</span>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>

        <a class="section-link" href="/publications/#technology">View all publications →</a>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <span>© 2026 Fernando Miramontes Forattini</span>
      <a href="/">Home</a>
    </footer>

    <script>
      import "../scripts/scroll-story.js";
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
